\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We placed twenty-eight deployed randomness failures, from Netscape in 1996 to the 2026 wallet
thefts, on a pipeline that follows SP 800-90 for the generator and adds consumption and the build
and integration surface, and we graded each against fourteen remedy families under stated rules.
The matrix shows that statistical testing and certification prevent none of these failures, that
only build-path verification guards the build surface, and that for most incidents a preventing
remedy existed before the system shipped. The research problems lie where the matrix is empty:
verifying the build path, certifying unpredictability rather than uniformity, and bringing
implementation security into randomness assurance. The practical problem is one of defaults,
tooling, and incentives.
